{"timestamp": 1778199287.6462753, "duration_sec": 2.5516350269317627, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Yeah, so you say interesting, and I'm like, well, these things are interesting, but maybe on a certain level. So one of the things I thought of was, like, an API so that you can have the option on Mac to have your app startup on startup for the user. So I don't know how exciting that is for everybody. But, like, that's a new and interesting thing that people are using online."], [0, "So is that something that someone might put like in a preferences window or something and they can like check it and that'll set it up to be something that starts on? Right. Yeah, exactly. So previously on Mac, it was still the user could manually go into their settings and go into the startup tab and select your app. And so it was possible for the user to manually do it that way. But now there's an API in Electron so that you can actually add that into your app's preferences."], [0, "Awesome. What is kind of like the installation story like when you're bundling up an app that someone needs to be able to install? Like I know the Electron apps that I've used on my Mac, I feel like they all just come as like .app files and they're kind of just self-contained and I open them and it's fine. On Windows and stuff, is there like an installation process or are there some people developing Electron apps on Mac that need to be like one of those PKG files that actually has like a wizard or anything like that?"], [0, "Yeah, for Mac, you just deliver the .app file. But for Windows, you can do a Windows installer or you can just do the straight exe. So it's"], [0, "It's up to you and your app. Cool. Yeah. So I guess getting back to the APIs and stuff, you mentioned that cool new one for being able to specify that an app launches at startup. What are kind of some of the kind of standard ones that Electron kind of came with out of the box? So we can maybe just talk about those a little bit for people who haven't worked with Electron before and are interested in what you can do with it. Yeah. So the most standard ones, I would say, are the ones around"], [0, "Your basic operating system functions like open a file, save a file, display an error or display a message dialogue with an option. Having access to those native UI elements is really like core core."], [0, "electron things, right? So you can have a file and you can save it to the user's file system and you can give them that native interface where it asks them, you know, it pops up the dialogue and asks them to name the file and hit save and that kind of thing. So your app is fully integrated with their operating system."], [0, "Just wanted to take a quick break to thank Rollbar for continuing to sponsor Full Stack Radio. So one of the frustrating things about being a developer is dealing with errors, of course, right? You know, either you rely on your users to report errors or you're digging through log files trying to figure out what went wrong. Or maybe you're hooked up to an existing tool and you've got millions of alerts flooding your inbox all day long. Rollbar is like a full stack error monitoring solution. And with Rollbar, you get the context, insights, and control that you need to find and fix bugs fast."], [0, "faster with a lot less noise. So a robot is really easy to install. You can start tracking production errors and deployments in eight minutes or less. It works with all major languages and frameworks, including Ruby, Python, JavaScript, PHP, Node, iOS. You know, you get the picture."], [0, "If you're a Laravel developer like myself, there's actually a package that you can use that integrates with Rollbar really quickly. So Rollbar also integrates with a lot of different other tools, like it can send your errors to Slack or HipChat or create new issues in GitHub, Jira, and stuff like that. And for Fullstack Radio listeners, Rollbar actually has a special offer where if you sign up at rollbar.com slash fullstack radio, you get access to their bootstrap plan for free for 90 days. So you get like 300,000 errors tracked for free."]], "participants": []}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["electron", "desktop apps", "application programming", "macos", "windows", "app startup", "user preferences", "installation", "file system", "native interface", "user interface", "error handling", "error monitoring", "debugging", "software development"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778199773.0283391, "duration_sec": 2.06744647026062, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "We can't have health care. We can't fund our schools. We can't build roads and bridges or anything like that. We can't have money for that. But when it comes to building Donald Trump's ballroom, Lindsey Graham and the Republicans are right there. They will do it the second he demands it. And Trump world sure does indeed seem obsessed with that ballroom. And in fact, Lindsey Graham is now saying it's taxpayers who should pay for it."], [0, "We're going to introduce legislation that would authorize $400 million to be spent to build the presidential ballroom. I'd like to vote as soon as possible to accelerate what America needs, a secure facility for the president and others to meet in, to have a good time."], [0, "accelerate what America needs or accelerate what Trump wants. Graham seems to think that it is a good idea to ask taxpayers, the vast majority of whom, by the way, will never get the chance to step foot in this ballroom to foot the bill. Mind you, last year, Trump told us all that he would pay for it himself. It would actually be a beautiful addition. It's going to cost nothing. I'll spend the whole thing myself. We'll do a big, beautiful room, but we could use it at the White House."], [0, "it's really all starting to feel a little bit like let them eat cake. With this news arriving as ProPublica reports, the White House is pushing for a rule change that would slash or end benefits for as many as 400,000 Americans on supplemental security income who are living with disabilities like Down syndrome or dementia and whose relatives receive SNAP benefits."], [0, "Yeah, so Republicans might not say it to the president, but they know this is creating a brutal environment about six months away from the midterms. And joining me now is Howard Dean, former Vermont governor and DNC chair. So it is good to see you. Do you think Americans should be helping foot the $400 million bill for a ballroom while they're looking at $418 for some gas?"], [0, "That's kind of a no-brainer for everybody except Trump, who evidently doesn't have a brain to worry about. Look, all this stuff, generally speaking, when a party is in power and in the kind of trouble that Trump's is, the deadline for turning it around is September. I think Trump's doing everything he can to make sure the deadline's next week."], [0, "He's not going to get out of this, unless he steals the election, which I know he's going to try to do. But I don't see how he turns this around. The only question in my mind is whether we're going to pick up two chambers or one."], [0, "So, I mean, what do you think the calculus is inside the minds of whether it's, you know, Trump and his cabinet officials or it's Lindsey Graham? I mean, what calculus can they be making here that voters simply will forget about these optics and the symbolism of a ballroom while they're struggling to put food on the table and to fill up their tanks? And that instead they're going to judge the Republican Party on what? I mean, like how well the Iran war is going? Like what what math is it?"], [0, "that they're doing here ahead of these midterms. It's just self-preservation. The Republican Party has become the party of hate and anger and obeying Trump. And right now, the electorate isn't going to put up with the Republican Party. I think they know that. So they're just getting what they can in Washington while the getting's good."], [0, "So I have to ask you about Trump's State Department, this new reporting that they're reportedly planning to put Trump's face on American passports. I mean, he wants this new arch to go up in D.C. The ballroom is somewhat underway. You have this. It is a lot to process, I think, for your average voter. What did you think when you saw that?"]], "participants": []}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["us politics", "republican party", "donald trump", "government spending", "public funding", "white house", "midterm elections", "social welfare", "disability benefits", "food assistance", "healthcare policy", "infrastructure", "political corruption", "voter backlash", "taxpayer money"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778199863.2521145, "duration_sec": 2.355928897857666, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "that they're doing here ahead of these midterms. It's just self-preservation. The Republican Party has become the party of hate and anger and obeying Trump. And right now, the electorate isn't going to put up with the Republican Party. I think they know that. So they're just getting what they can in Washington while the getting's good."], [0, "So I have to ask you about Trump's State Department, this new reporting that they're reportedly planning to put Trump's face on American passports. I mean, he wants this new arch to go up in D.C. The ballroom is somewhat underway. You have this. It is a lot to process, I think, for your average voter. What did you think when you saw that?"], [0, "Yeah, I just I think the guy's crazy. I mean, I thought that for a while. And I think the problem is he has a real hold over about 23 percent of the American people. But that's about 50 percent of the Republican Party. And the people who work for him and the people in Congress who think they work for him are, you know, they've long since given up doing anything for the constituents and they're just saving their own butts."], [0, "But in the end, it's not going to work. The American people have had it, and I don't blame them. Now the question is, can we whip the Democrats into shape so that we can present a colorful alternative and a capable alternative? I like very much the youth movement that's going on in the Democratic Party with a whole new generation starting to take power and challenge incumbents."], [0, "Interesting. Tell me more about that. The role that you think that youth movement is going to play in articulating what the Democratic Party is next, because, you know, there's this debate, right, that's been going on about whether it's enough for Democrats to basically let Trump make the case for them himself, you know, that he's going to keep making these blunders. The economy is, you know, faltering and people are in pain. So it'll just they'll be the obvious choice. But"], [0, "There are also a lot of young people in the Democratic Party who say, no, we need to actively make an argument, tell a story about who we are and what we're going to offer. If you look at the numbers of the Democratic Party, they're not that great. One of the reasons I thought that Mamdani's win in New York was so important was not that I agree with his politics. It says he was put into office by 25 and 35 year olds. And that's starting to happen all over the country."], [0, "I'm going to be fascinated now that Swalwell is out of the governor's race in California. Who wins that race on the Democratic side? It's just I think this new generation is taking power. And whether we like it or not, that's going to happen."], [0, "And there are going to be a lot of surprises. There are going to be a lot of veterans who get beat in primaries and don't come back. And I think there's a whole... The inside of the bellwake crowd has had it. I think people are fed up with them. Democrats and the Republicans, of course, have completely given up. But the Democrats got to earn this comeback. They can't just sit and wait, let the Republicans destroy themselves. We've got to..."], [0, "40-something-year-old guy, Zach Wall, is running out in Iowa. He's two points ahead of the Republican candidate in Iowa for the United States Senate. We're going to win the governor's race in Iowa. I mean, this is unbelievable. Nebraska, we've got a great chance in Omaha. I mean, I think we're going to knock off Susan Collins, who's so concerned about everything and never votes on both sides unless the Republicans say you've got to vote with us."], [0, "There's a lot of big people, big names that are going to go down in this election, but we have to show them, give them a reason to go down. And it can't just be Trump as a nutcase. Howard Dean, there are going to be a lot of interesting races to talk to you about in the weeks and months to come. Thanks so much. Thank you."]], "participants": []}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["us politics", "elections", "midterms", "republican party", "democratic party", "donald trump", "state department", "government", "voter sentiment", "youth movement", "political campaigns", "party leadership", "congressional races", "american voters", "political polarization"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778199886.5438812, "duration_sec": 1.656540870666504, "validator_hotkey": "5HmkM6X1D3W3CuCSPuHhrbYyZNBy2aGAiZy9NczoJmtY25H7", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "99% Invisible is supported by Claude from Anthropic. There's a design decision happening right now that most people won't notice until it's too late. AI is about to get ads. You ask a question and the architecture of the response, what gets prioritized, what gets left out, starts being shaped by who paid for placement. Anthropic just committed to keeping Claude ad-free. No sponsored responses, no third-party influence shaping what you receive. Try Claude for free at Claude.ai/99invisible. 99invisible and see why problem solvers choose Claude as their thinking partner."], [1, "Time is valuable."], [0, "That's why Lowe's Blueprint Takeoffs turn blueprints into quotes faster. Bring us your plans and we'll generate itemized material lists to make quoting easier so you can get back to building. Plus, at the Lowe's Pro Desk, you get access to thousands of building materials not sold in store, and when your."], [1, "Order is ready, We'll deliver everything to the job site."], [0, "Improving is easy at Lowe's. This is 99% Invisible. I'm Roman Mars. The walk begins at the parking lot at the end of the road. The site isn't much to speak of, just a sprawling expanse of asphalt peppered with cars. Over a million people come here every year to embark on a kind of pilgrimage. They're here to cross the border."], [1, "We are getting ready to go to Mexico this morning. Yes, one more time. So we shall see what the rest of the day holds. There are hundreds of videos online documenting this experience. Watch as many as I did and they all start to meld together."], [0, "That's 99 PI producer Lashima Dawn."], [1, "Some people seem nervous in the videos. Others are more excited. Many will turn to their imagined audience behind the camera and speak in a reassuring tone. You see that American flag up there, y'all? This is one of the safest crossings there is. And it's not scary, I promise you. It is not scary. Everyone here is headed to the same town in Mexico. It's called Los Algodones, and it's a place where, for decades, Americans have roamed the streets. They come here in desperate search of something they need, something that's almost impossible to find where they live. They're looking for a dentist, one that they can afford."], [0, "Every day, thousands of people, mostly American, mostly white, and mostly retired, come to this town looking for relief from the turmoil in their mouths. Here, dental care costs up to 80% less than what it might cost in the US. Dental tourism to Los Algodones is so common that many call this place Molar City. Today, there are nearly 1,000 dentists in this 7,000-person town, and 98% of those in the patient's chair traveled here from outside Mexico. Lasha is going to take it from here."], [1, "Watch any one of these YouTube videos, and you'll see how a trip to Molar City is made easy for an American. For decades, this crossing didn't even require a passport. In one video, a retired couple arrives at Arizona's edge. They do a 360, displaying the massive lot where they've just parked. \"We love Mexico,\" they say emphatically, talking to the camera. The camera then pans to show the rust-colored border wall in front of them. That corrugated monstrosity. They marvel. Then a US Border Patrol vehicle starts driving past the screen, on guard, it seems, for anyone trying to cross in the opposite direction. To this, the camera woman speaks again, as if she's talking directly to Border Patrol. \"Get 'em, boys,\" she says. Then they begin their walk into Mexico. In Los Algodones, there are dense clusters of medical offices and a canopy of billboards above them. Vendors are set up on sidewalks where they sell handmade goods and kitschy souvenirs, like this one graphic tee that says, \"Keep calm, you're on the fun side of Trump's wall.\" And then there are the street promoters, people who are stationed at almost every street corner. Each of them competing for superlatives: the best deal, the quickest appointment, the most popular clinic in town. In Molar City, a street promoter is the first person a tourist will encounter."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Society & Culture"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["podcast", "advertising", "artificial intelligence", "ethics", "border crossing", "mexico", "tourism", "dental care", "health costs", "medical travel", "retirees", "urban design", "marketing", "consumer services", "immigration"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
