ident Donald Trump weighing sending small special operations units into Iran as one option to prevent further enrichment. If ground operations were to occur, they would involve small raids rather than a large force. Two options reportedly presented were extracting the uranium from Iran or diluting it on location, with the final decision to be made by the President, the War Department and the CIA. Officials estimate Iran may possess about 450 kilograms of 60% enriched uranium,"], [0, "potentially enough for 11 bombs within weeks, and another option being considered is seizing Karg Island. In regional impacts, security forces were dispatched to the village of Kerbat Abu Efele Letewon E25 after reports of Israelis attacking Palestinians in the area. With the IDF and border police dispersing those involved, two Palestinians were killed by gunfire and another by asphyxiation."], [0, "and the incident is being investigated by Central Command, with oversight from the regional command and police. The report also notes additional tensions in the region, including an Israeli strike at Isfahan airport, fragments from interceptors found in the Shefela, and references to a broader narrative of heightened activity, including a nighttime operation tied to Iran's nuclear program, outreach to Beirut, alarms in the north and interceptions in the Gulf, all within a context of pressure on Iran's oil assets."], [0, "In the conflict with Iran and its regional proxies, Lebanon-related violence is reported with around 20 people killed in an attack on the village of Sir Al Arabiya in southern Lebanon. A figure echoed by Lebanese sources describing fatalities in the morning strike on a southern village. In US policy concerning Israel, a White House-affiliated official account responds to an anti-American journalist, taking a stance on the reporter's questions."], [0, "China's foreign ministry urged the United States and Israel to halt Iran strikes and warned that the war could spill over into other regions, potentially igniting a larger conflict. Former President Donald Trump asserted that the war against Iran after October 7 had to be done, and that the world had been aided by the action. In Israeli domestic politics, two miners, aged 16 and 17, were arrested in Jerusalem on suspicion of vandalism,"], [0, "and causing heavy damage at a public shelter in the Kataman neighbourhood, a protected space for residents nearby. Police emphasised zero tolerance for vandalism or damage to emergency infrastructure, and said the investigation would be pursued to bring those responsible to justice, with the incident underscoring ongoing security concerns amid the crisis environment."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["News & Politics"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["middle east conflict", "israel iran war", "air strikes", "military operations", "fighter jets", "air force", "nuclear weapons", "uranium enrichment", "regime change", "security forces", "lebanon violence", "international relations", "united states policy", "domestic politics", "human rights"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778205670.4788234, "duration_sec": 1.6625339984893799, "validator_hotkey": "5HmkM6X1D3W3CuCSPuHhrbYyZNBy2aGAiZy9NczoJmtY25H7", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Was only $30 million. Ticket, or you mean within corporation? What do you mean when you say."], [1, "They\u2014 his company bought a ticket for the lottery? Yes, Zorro Trust, which is his company."], [0, "Company."], [1, "They won the lottery, which is very suspicious. Not only that, he won the lottery right after he was arrested and went to jail for fucking kids or having sex or whatever, whatever he was arrested for\u2014 sexual handjobs, whatever it was."], [0, "So there's nothing wrong with underage\u2014 no."], [1, "But when they're underage girls, probably not."], [0, "Oh, I didn't know that part."], [1, "So then when he went, um, and got the lottery money, the company took the payoff. The payoff out of an $80 million payment, the $80 million jackpot, was only $30 million. So if you want the money, you take 30. So not only did they take 50 out of the 80, but then you think about how many people spent money buying lottery tickets. It's way more than $80 million. So they make money off of that, and then they make money off of the fact that you want the payoff instead of the, you know, the over\u2014."], [0, "So they never could\u2014 it doesn't matter what the jackpot becomes, they never can lose."], [1, "They can't lose because they're stealing money from people that are desperate. It's It's legalized gambling where the house always wins. Right. Like, let's find that out. Like, let's say, let's find an average jackpot of Mega Bucks and find out how much money actually goes into it, how much, how much money people spend versus how much money the payout is."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["lottery", "gambling", "jackpot", "winnings", "payout", "trust", "corporation", "fraud", "suspicion", "crime", "sexual abuse", "underage girls", "jail", "money", "legalized gambling"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778205945.9243994, "duration_sec": 3.0289347171783447, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Going off of that a little bit, Mr. Mayor, the Denver Public Schools Committee, a Denver Public Schools Committee is considering a ban on phones from the start of the school day to the end of the school day for all students. As a former teacher, principal yourself, is this something would you like to see a full outright ban of phones or is there any way of balancing a use in schools?"], [0, "I think it's a fantastic idea. I would support. I mean, during the school day, eight to four, there's no, I think, you know, critical use for it. And what there is, is I think a real need to not just have kids develop longer attention spans of focus, but it is also about social development, right? What we know is a challenge for young people is if..."], [0, "You can be nervous as a middle schooler, and you don't know how to, and it's awkward, or as an elementary school student. But that ability to have to walk into a conversation with a peer or a teacher is what helps you develop that self-confidence. When you have the crutch of being able to retreat to your phone all the time, it doesn't allow you to go to the discomfort of that development and growth. And so I fully agree."], [0, "The idea is if we could, from the city level, try to push some of those kind of changes because we think the more chances kids have to focus on instruction and relationship building rather than technology is great. There are other ways to use technology in the classrooms, of course, that we should use, but I don't think they require individual student phone use for the things that they use it for mostly, which is social media."], [0, "Let's talk about crime for a moment, Mayor Mike Johnston. As you'll recall a few weeks back when I had the opportunity to be with you and Gina together, I told you that I'm a three-time victim of auto theft, and I've been pleased to see Denver getting tougher in that regard. We talked about the accent cameras, and you won that push."], [0, "to make sure that you have those surveillance opportunities available for law enforcement, which are key in tracking down vehicles often used, stolen cars, in the commission of other crimes. So I wonder, as an advocate for implementing anti-crime tech and the husband of a prosecutor, I know that you get..."], [0, "that we need to put bad guys in jail so they don't harm the rest of us. But then I see some of the things that are happening at the legislature with bills like the one that would water down first degree murder, which I simply cannot wrap my head around that one. I wonder if you think that or worry at all that the pendulum might have swung a little bit too far in the name of justice reform, including among some folks in your party, but in general."], [0, "I think it has. I think, frankly, there are places where I think we've overreacted on wanting to protect against, you know, over-enforcement or over-incarceration. I think there are lots of good policies we know prevent over-incarceration, particularly people of color. I'll tell you, the best policy of all is putting more officers in the street. So that is the best thing to do is it's prevention, which prevents people from ever being charged, prevents crimes from happening. So that's what..."], [0, "We've been focusing on here in Denver, and that's really made a difference because everyone wants to see more officers there to be able to help prevent crime. So we focus on that. I don't follow all the stuff in the legislature, but I think people that believe that what the public want right now is massive reductions and accountability for folks who are breaking the law, I think they're out of touch with where the public is. That is not what folks want. They want to see the law enforced. They want to know that they feel safe in their city. And that means, I think, more officers and more accountability. And so that's where I am, and that's where I think most residents of Denver are."], [0, "The story we've had in the newscast for the past couple of weeks now, we've been really following that city council delay of the next steps in the approval process for the e-scooter and e-bike programs in the city. Curious if you support this proposed VO contract with this new e-scooter company and getting rid of the limes and the birds that we have scattered throughout the city."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["News & Politics"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["education policy", "school discipline", "mobile phones", "student attention", "social development", "technology in classrooms", "social media", "city government", "crime prevention", "policing", "criminal justice", "surveillance cameras", "auto theft", "public safety", "urban transportation"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778206292.1201773, "duration_sec": 2.7869503498077393, "validator_hotkey": "5C8LULGoFybR7ybg4S8qQkaaowGXFFja4KmMoSqwhxuAsn33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[3, "They really\u2014 they really only come when they have to."], [1, "Yeah."], [3, "The reason they don't like it is because of the immigration, the way they treat them when they're crossing. They always get harassed. Yeah. When they're going back to America, they get harassed. So they get tired of that."], [1, "Los Algodones has long been set up for the benefit of Americans, and the journey Americans take to cross into Molar City has always been a simple one. But of course, the journey in the opposite direction is much more complicated. Can I ask you a question that might sound\u2014 I don't know, I don't know how you'll take it, but I'll ask it anyway. You can tell me how you react to it. I've heard from Americans and Canadians, like people describing what it's like to go down to Los Algodones, and people describe just walking freely through the border, like just 5 minutes, no need to even show a passport. And I, I'm just curious what that's like for you on your end, knowing that you were kicked out, and yet part of your job is to interact daily with I don't know, hundreds of people who just walk so freely across this border, you know?"], [3, "The way I see it is like this. Since I am from the border and we lived in America, we went to school in America, what happens is that when I see people coming from America and I see myself that I can't go back, which is like literally Right there, I can see my mom's house from right here."], [1, "Oh, wow."], [3, "That's the hardest thing to see. I see my house. I got the Colorado River, and I can literally see the water tank at the reservation, and then I can see all the way to the Yuma hospital on 24th Street, and then I kind of know more or less where my mom's house is over here. If I had a drone, I could fly it over there and and look at and go, \"Ouch.\"."], [1, "Yeah."], [3, "That's how close I am. That's not easy. It's very hard for me."], [1, "It's 5:00 AM in Algodones. The sky is still dark, not quite sunrise, and Alberto is shuffling around the house, getting ready for for work. The streets are mostly quiet at this hour, just the sound of other promoters and vendors setting up, setting the stage for Molar City. By 6:00 AM, he's at his post. The border opens and the Americans descend. They walk in the shadow of the 30-foot wall that divides them. Crossing the very threshold Alberto cannot."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Society & Culture"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["immigration", "border crossing", "mexico", "united states", "deportation", "citizenship", "travel", "discrimination", "identity", "family separation", "border wall", "indigenous people", "daily life", "inequality", "labor"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778206403.8459074, "duration_sec": 1.7597126960754395, "validator_hotkey": "5HmkM6X1D3W3CuCSPuHhrbYyZNBy2aGAiZy9NczoJmtY25H7", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[3, "Well, I got them all, you know, I got them all pointed out. I got a report on every single one of them. Which one would you want me to start with? I got\u2014 my competition's everywhere. Everybody's my competition."], [1, "There are dozens of street promoters like Alberto, and because there are so many of them, all working on commission, Alberto's success depends on his charisma. He has to give the best performance. And so Alberto has his own strategies to try to stand out and make a good impression. Besides workshopping comedic material on the job, he told me this one other thing. He tends to play up how American he is. It's not a hard thing for him to do because like many people who grew up along the border, Alberto traveled back and forth a lot. He lived in Arizona but crossed the border weekly. His English is good. He describes himself as being \"from the border.\"."], [3, "So I grew up, you know, going to school and then coming down the weekends down to Mexico. And a lot of kids grew up like that. Because there's a lot of people in Agua Dulce that were\u2014 oh, I forgot to mention I was deported."], [1, "Alberto was deported to Mexico in 2010. In fact, I learned that almost all of Molar City's street promoters are deportees. It is in fact their Americanness, their American English, that makes them ideal candidates for this role."], [3, "It helped me break the ice. It helps me bond with people. Right away they can tell that I lived in America."], [1, "Oh, they bring it up?"], [3, "Yeah, they'll say it like that. They'll be like, \"Oh, I can hear you. Oh yeah, you lived in the States, huh?\" I'm like, \"Yes. Got me a little agent McCasian over there.\" So it makes them\u2014 makes me relate to them more and feel more relaxed."], [1, "Alberto tells me he'll recognize familiar faces crossing into Mexico from time to time. So-and-so from high school, his friend's uncle. But he doesn't get to see his family so often. They all live in Arizona and don't like to come visit."], [3, "They really\u2014 they really only come when they have to."], [1, "Yeah."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Society & Culture"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["immigration", "deportation", "border life", "mexico", "united states", "street marketing", "commission work", "sales", "competition", "charisma", "performance", "identity", "bilingualism", "family separation", "cross border travel"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778206633.507391, "duration_sec": 1.805452823638916, "validator_hotkey": "5FZe9Mpo5dLXfhGZ4VjRnw6dmz9LVPp6nzqPyDfaS9BLKs33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "A female handler. They call it geriatric. Like, this is what I hear. This is what the streets are saying. Most men get to an age, it's a geriatric shit when you just smash all the women you want to do and everything. Now you're going to have to worry about somebody helping you with your pill diet, helping you with your dietary needs and everything. And they say that's a lot of times when men fall in love, when they need somebody to take them to the golden years. Or when you're about to be out of here, you need I've heard somebody say, don't do that. You gotta mash this food up, you gotta chop it up. But I'm having digestive issues sometimes."], [1, "With steak, huh?"], [0, "It's red meat, I wanna say. And I'm a fan of it."], [1, "So if you eat like a bowl of pasta with the Tito's, no problem?"], [0, "That's not a problem."], [1, "Interesting."], [0, "But it's definitely red meat."], [1, "You should go to one of those doctors that checks people for allergies."], [0, "Voodoo doctor?"], [1, "Yeah."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["aging", "elderly care", "men", "love", "relationships", "diet", "nutrition", "digestion", "red meat", "food intolerance", "allergies", "health", "medical testing", "pasta", "alcohol"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778206726.6313782, "duration_sec": 1.4934170246124268, "validator_hotkey": "5FZe9Mpo5dLXfhGZ4VjRnw6dmz9LVPp6nzqPyDfaS9BLKs33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "So you're actually getting high off of cigarettes?"], [1, "100%. I get high off of them because I don't smoke them all the time. So I only smoke\u2014 if I smoke a couple cigarettes a week, it's a lot."], [0, "And Newport's probably like 10 times worse than, uh, menthol cigarettes, probably 10 times worse for you about it."], [1, "I was talking to Cat about that. Yeah, I was asking him like, why, why do you like menthols? And he's like, he was speaking on behalf of the black community. He said we like things that are more potent."], [0, "I believe that that's possible part of it, but I also believe that back in the day, and this supply\u2014 I don't know if I talked about this\u2014 it was certain brands that targeted certain communities just for the loyalty of it."], [1, "Yeah."], [0, "And I think Newport was targeting\u2014 I don't know if it was a situation when Newport came out, they were spending more ad money with advertising everything because I don't know if I shared this story with you, but, um, uh, Pepsi was a company that did that where they targeted the Black community. So I think even though I understand we said more potent, but I think it was something that was in our community, whether that was like cheaper prices or whatever. And I think it's generation and generation, like, you need to do this because Black people did this because it was cheaper. I think that that might be the case with Newport."], [1, "Yeah, probably both. What is the, the menthol effect? What, what is the What is the difference?"], [0, "I know when I smoke menthol, I sound like Katt Williams the next day. They ruined\u2014 that's one of the reasons I had to downgrade to like\u2014 some people think that I started smoking Marlboro Lights because I started dating white women, which is more appealing. Unless you date a white woman from the Midwest, then she probably smoking Newports and drinking Pepsis and Coca-Cola just like you. But I think that's probably\u2014 but I got so many bad habits that I need to change."], [1, "Here we go. And our sponsor, our AI sponsor Perplexity, says the menthol effect in cigarettes come from the chemical menthol itself, which is added as a flavoring and a sensory agent to the tobacco. Menthol is naturally found in peppermint and other mint plants, can also be made synthetically in a lab. Menthol activates cold-sensitive nerve receptors in the mouth, throat, and airways, creating a cooling sensation when you inhale smoke.. It's a mild anesthetic numbing effect that reduces pain and irritation from hot, harsh cigarette smoke, making it feel smoother. Menthol can suppress the cough reflex and dull early warnings or early warning signs of airway irritation, which make it easier to inhale more deeply and more often. Menthol reduces the perceived harshness of nicotine and smoke. The minty taste and smell plus the cooling feel act as pleasant sensory cues that many smokers come to associate with satisfaction and craving. Menthol can alter nicotine metabolism and the way nicotine acts on brain receptors, which may increase nicotine's reinforcing addictive effects. In short, the menthol effect is not from nicotine but from added menthol, which cools and numbs the airways, masks irritation, and can make cigarettes feel smoother and more addictive without making them any safer."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["smoking", "cigarettes", "nicotine", "menthol", "addiction", "tobacco", "health risks", "marketing", "advertising", "consumer behavior", "race", "black community", "culture", "substance use", "public health"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
