{"timestamp": 1778208036.4824424, "duration_sec": 2.009962797164917, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "kind of take all the lessons learned that gathered from my discussions with Brian about the challenges that nonprofits and NGOs face and build a program around that to address those. And for those who aren't necessarily familiar with the Global Water Challenge, could you kind of talk a little bit about what that is in general before we fully dive into how you were interacting with that, just so that people who haven't heard of it before have a reference point?"], [0, "Sure. Yeah. So the Global Water Challenge is a nonprofit based here in Washington, D.C., with the mission of helping bring water to communities in developing nations. So roughly one in four people around the world are dependent on nontraditional water sources like hand pumps or wells. And the Global Water Challenge sets out to help direct investment to countries in need."], [0, "And so is that investment, are those projects seeking to upgrade those water systems? Or just when you say it's not available, I guess what I'm trying to work through is it sounds like part of it is maybe clean water and good sources, and part of it is just access at all? Or what sort of projects do they work on? Yeah, they have a wide portfolio, but"], [0, "The ones that we've been focusing on with DataRobot have been around typically new construction and rehabilitation of water points. So for example, you have a community that may not have an infrastructure for running water. So they may look to direct investment to drill new wells or repair new pumps for people in these communities. So you'll have villages that don't have access to water and they will help direct investment with other NGOs or large corporations."], [0, "to either drill or build new water points. Yeah. Yeah. And I guess this problem has probably become increasingly evident, even more so over these recent times, because I guess, you know, of course, disease spreads in various ways. But if people aren't able to access water and, you know, for cleaning and all of those things, then I'm sure it further exacerbates many things related to disease spread and health and a lot of different things."], [0, "Yeah, absolutely. I mean, particularly now with the importance of hand-washing, having access to clean water has never been more important. So yeah, the downstream effects of not having clean water in a community are huge when it even comes to impacts in equality and education, as well as just overall disease. Being able to move the needle on that has huge impacts for an economy. And I think one place that was really exciting about the Global Water Challenge and what drew us to work with them"], [0, "was their focus on data. So around the world, there are hundreds of thousands of these water points, but there is no centralized repository. And Brian from the Global Water Challenge set out to build the first standardized and normalized database for water points around the world."], [0, "We deserve a better internet, and the Brave team has the recipe for bringing it to us. Start with Google Chrome. Keep the extensions, the dev tools, and the rendering engine that make Chrome great. Rip out the Google bits. We don't need them. Mix in ad and tracker blocking by default. Quick access to the Tor network for true private browsing and an opt-in reward system so you can get paid to view privacy-respecting ads. Then turn around and use those rewards to support your favorite web creators like us."], [0, "Download Brave today using the link in the show notes and give tipping a try on changelog.com."], [0, "So Chandler, I'm interested, you just started talking about the focus of data that Global Water Challenge has. I assume that that focus on data and the data that they've gathered in this repository is central to the solution that you built for them. But I was wondering if you could maybe describe that data a little bit more. What does it represent? What is the scale of it? And what sort of information is included in that data?"]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["nonprofits", "ngo", "clean water", "water access", "global development", "developing countries", "public health", "disease prevention", "sanitation", "infrastructure", "water wells", "data collection", "database", "investment", "data science", "social impact"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778208210.3136601, "duration_sec": 1.9587125778198242, "validator_hotkey": "5C8LULGoFybR7ybg4S8qQkaaowGXFFja4KmMoSqwhxuAsn33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[2, "Agua Dulce was a very small town with lots of cantinas, and used to come people from Yuma to dance in the cantinas or drink."], [1, "This is Dr. Jesus Medina. He moved to town fresh out of med school in 1973, and he's been working here as a doctor ever since, although he seems to be perpetually on the brink of retirement."], [2, "I think one day I have to retire. And my sons tell me, \"When you gonna retire?\"."], [0, "Why?"], [2, "I'm too young to retire."], [1, "Dr. Medina first learned about Los Algodones from his brother-in-law, Dr. Bernardo Maga\u00f1a, who's often described as the godfather of Molar City."], [2, "He was the first dentist from this town, you know?"], [1, "Maga\u00f1a's now in his 80s. He is retired and didn't want to be interviewed. But Medina told me about how his brother-in-law Maga\u00f1a first had this vision to transform the town into a dental mecca. Back in the '60s, Maga\u00f1a and Medina were living together in a city about 25 miles from Algodones. Maga\u00f1a was a young dentist looking for a place to set up his private practice. At the time, he was working for someone who already had a few American patients. Patients who likely lived in the South and would cross into Mexico to take advantage of lower prices."], [2, "Gladstone started to tell about Algodones. He says, \"Why don't you move to Algodones? Because Algodones is 4 minutes from Yuma.\" He says, \"If you go there, you're going to have a lot more patients.\"."], [1, "At first, Maga\u00f1a was skeptical. He paid Los Algodones a visit, and it was a lot like he expected. A sleepy little place with a lot of mostly drunk people. But many of those drunk people were Americans. Its proximity to the border meant there was already a culture of coming and going. He saw the Americans spending their money at the bars and thought maybe they'd spend money on dentistry too. Maga\u00f1a knew he could offer them a price lower than what they could get in America."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Society & Culture"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["border towns", "mexico", "united states", "dental tourism", "dentistry", "medical practice", "doctors", "retirement", "small towns", "cantinas", "nightlife", "cross border travel", "healthcare costs", "private practice", "local history"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778208235.6563983, "duration_sec": 1.9045546054840088, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Where do I host this thing? Where do I run it? And all of my enthusiasm for building this thing kind of just goes out the window. I'm like, actually, you know what? I'm fine doing this manual task. And one of the things that we saw internally as we were building prototypes is we were building a lot of these kinds of integrations. One of the things that we see people building a ton of both, you know, obviously within the company, but in our user base,"], [0, "is these Slack integrations, which are really fun, really easy to build, and made all the more powerful when you get to connect all the data that you care about. So a colleague of mine built this really neat Slack chatbot using Google's Dialogflow"], [0, "where he always gets confused in AWS CloudWatch about how to specify queries and whether it's UTC or local time zone and all that stuff. And so he built a bot where he can chat with it and say, hey, can you find the log"], [0, "this error, either in our staging environment or our production environment. And then Dialogflow, which is its own kind of magic, parses that NLP and then hands back to transposite the key nouns and verbs. And then his app goes and fetches the appropriate data out of CloudWatch. So we see a lot of those kinds of automating quality of life applications being built."], [0, "Unrelated to any of this, because you shared in the chat the documentation for MailChimp, I really like that you guys are using the native details and summary elements for your expand and collapse functionality on your website. Oh, cool. Developments being used for their intended purpose."], [0, "Yeah, good. That was done, you know, a day or two ago or whatever. So also that might be up your alley. We're using Eleventy. So I want to give a shout out to Eleventy. Oh, awesome. Yeah, Zach's static site generator. That's awesome. We're embarrassingly excited about it. Yeah, JS Jabber is hosted on Eleventy now too. So that's..."], [0, "Yeah, my 11pm to 2am project is usually all Eleventy based. That's awesome. Yeah, it's an awesome platform. I'd love to learn a little bit more about how this"], [0, "actually works under the hood. This seems like a huge undertaking. I know we've talked a little bit about Swagger slash LAS or OpenAPI specification, but what sort of other things do you need to make something like this work under the hood? Yeah, good question. And yeah, there are a ton of different pieces."], [0, "So first, part of the core of this thing is what we call the relational engine. It's this parser that takes the SQL and translates it into this intermediate form and executes it efficiently. So that's one big chunk of that. Our backend is all in Java and don't hate us for it, but we picked it kind of because it's boring."], [0, "And we have this very pragmatic attitude internally. I love PHP for the same reason. It's boring and it works. Yeah, exactly. You know, I think I made the mistake of telling my co-founder, you know, people like Java, but nobody loves Java. And it turns out she actually is in love with Java."]], "participants": []}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["software development", "slack bots", "chatbot", "automation", "application integration", "cloudwatch logs", "amazon web services", "natural language processing", "dialogflow", "sql", "openapi", "api documentation", "static site generator", "eleventy", "java backend"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778208321.7164562, "duration_sec": 1.6872079372406006, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "A partial collapse of a seven-level parking garage under construction near CHOP in Philadelphia's Grays Ferry neighborhood sent rescue teams to the scene around 2 o'clock p.m. on April 8, 2026, with one person confirmed dead and two others unaccounted for. Responders focused searches on the lower levels near elevator shafts and stairwells, delaying deeper searches until the structure could be stabilized due to safety concerns."], [0, "Arriving crews at the 3000 block of Grays Ferry Avenue initiated search and rescue operations, but questions about structural integrity hindered progress. Chopper 3 provided live aerial coverage and ongoing visuals as the response unfolded. City officials, Philadelphia police, and emergency management officials sought additional details while media outlets pursued more information."], [0, "Licenses and inspections inspectors were dispatched to the scene, and CHOP officials had not yet issued a statement. An inspector from licenses and inspections was reported en route to join the inquiry. The incident unfolded around 2 o'clock p.m. local time, with conditions limiting information on injuries and causes as responders worked."], [0, "The situation remained developing, with officials and reporters urging readers to check back for updates. Eyewitnesses described a loud collapse with rapid emergency response, noting that a man had already been pulled from the rubble by the time onlookers arrived."], [0, "Emergency communications at the scene were disrupted as rescue and investigative operations proceeded, and Fire Commissioner Jeffrey Thompson said stabilization of the compromised seven-story structure is essential before continuing searches to protect responders and potential victims."], [0, "A pro-Iranian hacking collective known as Handala has paused attacks on the United States for now. But officials say the group plans to resume targeting Israel and, when the time is right, to renew US strikes, underscoring how cyber operations are intertwined with physical conflict. Handala has previously claimed operations against Stryker, a Michigan-based medical equipment maker, and leaked emails from US figures after breaches, signaling a pattern of high-profile targets."], [0, "The group has exploited targets and leaked material tied to US and Israeli officials, prompting US authorities to seize related domain names and publicize breaches. Analysts expect future operations to be high profile and attention-grabbing, mirroring earlier disruptive incidents like the Stryker case."], [0, "The broader pattern shows cyber warfare as a persistent component of the conflict, with digital assaults continuing alongside traditional military actions and using proxies and multiple attack vectors. Experts foresee a surge in high-profile attacks designed to draw public attention, noting Iranian-backed cyber operations have been high volume but with relatively lower impact so far."], [0, "During lulls and kinetic hostilities, analysts expect cyber activity to expand in scale and scope, targeting U.S. organizations tied to the war effort, including data centers, tech firms, and defense contractors. Overall, expert assessments indicate pro-Iranian hackers have mounted high-volume operations to signal vulnerability and morale, even as military advantages persist."], [0, "Other pro-Iranian actors have deployed malware on mobile devices and conducted surveillance of cameras for missile targeting, alongside attacks on data centers and facilities in Israel, Saudi Arabia, and Kuwait. These actors have pursued attacks on Israelis and regional facilities, including attempts to compromise devices and data centers across Israel, Saudi Arabia, and Kuwait, highlighting a broad regional cyber threat landscape."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["News & Politics"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["breaking news", "construction accident", "building collapse", "parking garage", "search and rescue", "emergency response", "workplace safety", "structural engineering", "philadelphia", "cyber security", "hacking", "cyber warfare", "iran israel conflict", "critical infrastructure", "data breach"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778209540.168444, "duration_sec": 1.706496238708496, "validator_hotkey": "5C8LULGoFybR7ybg4S8qQkaaowGXFFja4KmMoSqwhxuAsn33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "And at that time you were already training with Maurice Smith."], [1, "Yes, Maurice was one of the guests."], [0, "Right, you were training. Yeah. Because I ended up training with Maurice Smith, you know, every time I'd go to Seattle. You know, we'd train together. So, you know, we're like, part of this like kind of karate martial art nerd culture."], [1, "Yeah."], [0, "When it wasn't even popular."], [1, "No."], [0, "I used to see you all the time. You know, you and, you know, doing jiu-jitsu, Carl Parisian and all these guys at Legends. Where was another place? It was, we had, there was Legends and there was another place."], [1, "The Bomb Squad."], [0, "Yes."], [1, "The Bomb Squad was the first place that Eddie taught at and then that place closed down, then we went to Legends. Yeah. And then we moved to the other Legends that was like in more East LA. Mm-hmm. And then And then Eddie started opening up his own place in downtown."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["martial arts", "karate", "jiu jitsu", "training", "gym", "fighting", "combat sports", "sparring", "coaching", "martial culture", "seattle", "los angeles", "dojo", "community", "fitness"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778210222.1551342, "duration_sec": 2.674466371536255, "validator_hotkey": "5FZe9Mpo5dLXfhGZ4VjRnw6dmz9LVPp6nzqPyDfaS9BLKs33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "No, no. But I mean\u2014 but there was so much There were so many events, things that would\u2014 I just call it on a Wednesday that I went through that it's like, I don't know, like, I think I wouldn't trade it because I continue to be the happiest guy I know because of, I think, some of that."], [1, "Because you can appreciate the good times."], [0, "Oh my God, yeah. And I should be slapped if I complain about anything. You know what I mean? Like, what? And so, you know, so I just\u2014 boy, I just know I'm so blessed. And, and you know what we do, what we're doing even right now, man, we're, we're in the service industry, man. You know, you're here to serve, in my opinion. That's what we're all here for. And, you know, it's great that we get to serve and doing the things that we would like to do. \u2014inspired us."], [1, "That's definitely a lot of what we do. I mean, that's definitely a lot of it, right? You entertain, but I feel very blessed that I've been able to expose people to so many different ways of thinking, so much information, so many different human beings that have led completely different paths that can tell you about whatever discipline they're involved in, what they've learned, what we're working on right now, and what you can learn about the human mind, the body, ancient history, be fill in the blank, like whatever it is."], [0, "Yeah, yeah. I mean, and I see you do that over and over, and you, in utmost honesty, I remember like where you had to kind of pull Shab aside and tell, as a friend, some things that are hard for people that, you know, other friends to tell him, you know. And like, that was real hard."], [1, "Yeah, well, that was real hard because I love that guy. He's a great, he's a great person. He's a great human And I knew the path. I'd seen it too many times. But I hadn't seen it with someone I was that close with. I was like, \"You have to stop.\" Because you\u2014 not only that, you're in the heavyweight division. So the knockouts are brutal. And you're gonna get 3 or 4 more in the next couple of years. And then you're not gonna recover from those."], [0, "But, man, so many people, I hope they take a page out of that. Because it's so non-manly, I feel like, to not\u2014 to just not say anything, right? And allow somebody you love to go down the road. I mean, that, that might be detrimental for them."], [1, "Well, it was also\u2014 Shobb had another path. He was really good at podcasting. He's fun. He's a funny dude, right? Yeah, he's like, as a podcast, he's like, got a great personality. He's hilarious. He's silly, you know, he's a big giant silly dude. Yeah, like, we would have so much fun, and he was doing really well, and he was making more money doing that than he was fighting. Yeah, but his identity was so wrapped up in him being a top 10 UFC heavyweight, right? Yeah, he had beat world-class guys like Mirko Cro Cop, you know, and he was legit, man. But though that time had passed, yeah, and I saw that his\u2014 he was one foot in and one foot out. And as soon as the guy's one foot in, one foot out, you're gonna run into some guy who has both feet in and is a fucking samurai, and then you're gonna wake up on a stretcher. Yeah, you're on the way to the hospital going, what happened? And you don't remember the fight, you don't remember nothing. And then you don't know where your keys are. You forget people's names. You tell the same story over and over again, and then you struggle to put sentences together. When you start seeing dudes with the slur\u2014 Nothing's worth that, man. Nothing's worth that. Because you're\u2014 I mean, at the time he's only 35 years old or whatever he was. I'm like, man, you got another 45, 55 years of life. You can't do this. You can't sacrifice all these years for glory that will never be achieved anyway because you're not on that path anymore."], [0, "Yeah, and it's not about what strangers say about you. No, it's about, you know, your friends, your family. Yeah, really love you."], [1, "It's just so hard for people to abandon that identity. That's the hardest thing with fighters is to abandon that identity. Mm-hmm. We've seen so many guys, even the greats, they come back and they shouldn't, and you see it, and you see them get humiliated."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["gratitude", "happiness", "personal growth", "service", "entertainment", "podcasting", "sports", "martial arts", "fighting", "athlete identity", "friendship", "mentorship", "honesty", "brain injury", "life after sports"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778210388.2133956, "duration_sec": 3.126420021057129, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Oh, my announcement is that I'm going to be a dad this summer, which I'm super excited about. Yeah, I'm scared too, but I think I'm mostly excited. Be prepared for a lack of sleep. That's all I can say. Yeah. My advice to everybody who's a first-time father, because now that I am somehow, I'm, I don't know, more aware of people that are or whatever, but if you don't throw the child out the window within the first three months, you are a good dad."], [0, "That's a pretty low bar. Yeah, I was going to say, that's a very low bar. Well, Steve, having had three kids, really, you think that's a low bar? You're not a psychopath. You're a good dad. No, it's not about being a psychopath. It's hard. I mean, like,"], [0, "they scream and like the lack of sleep. It's really a two person job. Like, I don't know how single parents do it. I, I certainly could not do it. I don't know. I don't know how the single moms do it. Like God bless them. God help them."], [0, "But if you can manage, never mind. You know what? You'll see what it is. And maybe you have an easy baby or maybe you don't. But if you know what I mean, then you'll know what I mean. I'll just leave it at that. My first one slept through the night at about six weeks. My second one is about two or three months. And my last one was a year."], [0, "Oh, wow. Fight of variability. Oh, my gosh. He was the kind where he'd cry and you finally get him to sleep. And as soon as you put him down, you jostle him just a bit and he's awake again. And, you know, my wife and I 